--TEST--
a class-scoped `type` alias is reached through the owner that declares it, so `Sub::Name` where only an ancestor declares `Name` is `E0405` naming the spelling that resolves — `rule:types/type-alias`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/type-alias`: a class-scoped alias is reached as `Owner::Name`
// from anywhere and as a bare `Name` inside the owner's own body, and through
// nothing else — not a subclass, not an implementor. The refusal names the
// owner that does declare it, because that is the spelling that resolves and
// the fix is to write it.
interface Contract {
    type Handle = array<uint>;
}

class Base {
    type Id = array<uint>;
}

class Sub extends Base {}

class Implementor implements Contract {}

// The accepted half first: through the declaring owner's own name, and as a
// bare name inside that owner's body.
class Uses {
    public static function throughTheOwner(Base::Id $ids): void {}

    public static function throughTheInterface(Contract::Handle $handle): void {}
}

class Reaches {
    type Id = array<uint>;

    public static function bare(Id $ids): void {}
}

class Refused {
    public static function throughASubclass(Sub::Id $ids): void {}

    public static function throughAnImplementor(Implementor::Handle $handle): void {}
}

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0405]: `Sub` has no `type` named `Id`
   --> case.nvs:34:45
%A
error[E0405]: `Implementor` has no `type` named `Handle`
   --> case.nvs:36:49
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
